WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:01.480
Is the camera rolling?

2
00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:02.300
The camera is rolling.

3
00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:42.180
Ever since the emergence of civilization people have left behind traces of their existence.

4
00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:47.700
Where does this need of ours come from to record something, document it, remember it?

5
00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:53.440
Whether we speak of drawings on cave walls, of donation temples and monuments,

6
00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.520
whether ceremonies of remembrance and celebration of important dates

7
00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.740
or modern documentary film — one thing is certain.

8
00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:05.400
We all strive to leave behind something by which we will be remembered.

9
00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:14.040
This film came about in Belgrade in 1895, or in 1927, or in 2020.

10
00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.040
It doesn't matter at all.

11
00:01:55.670 --> 00:02:02.550
I can't determine exactly the boundary when it began, let alone at what age.

12
00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:11.030
Because I remember discovering cinema from an early age, maybe when I was 7–8,

13
00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:15.970
I lived on Leka's Hill, a bit below the Central Prison.

14
00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:23.290
And we had a cinema there on Grčića Milenka street, called "Lekino brdo," and we called it the "Flea Pit."

15
00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:28.190
It had an oily floor, benches instead of seats.

16
00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:35.770
And I remember well that it had only one film projector, so in the middle of each film they had to take a break

17
00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:45.710
for the projectionist to change the reel, and the projectionist was a short man, a little hunchbacked.

18
00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:56.350
And when he cut the film and spliced it, he always had to cut off one or two frames from the film strip.

19
00:02:56.350 --> 00:03:06.130
He collected them and handed them out to children, and we gladly accepted them.

20
00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:15.470
And I remember that these pictures fascinated me so much that one day I found some lens,

21
00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:22.410
probably from some projector, and a box,

22
00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:29.490
and tried to make something like a projector to watch these pictures on the wall in my kitchen.

23
00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:36.350
I didn't realize then that cinema is in fact movement, time, rhythm and so on,

24
00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:43.190
but I imagined that these pictures could come alive on my wall.

25
00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:52.040
Also, every Friday my father bought me "Zabavnik," "Politikin Zabavnik,"

26
00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:57.480
and for himself he bought "Večernje novosti" every day.

27
00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:05.340
In "Večernje novosti" there was a comic called "Cisco Kid," a fictional character Cisco Kid.

28
00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:11.780
I loved that comic and simply devoured it, barely waiting for my father to bring the paper,

29
00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:21.020
and on Fridays, of course, I enjoyed "Politikin Zabavnik" and all the comics printed in it.

30
00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:26.140
In some imagination of mine, I can't explain to myself how,

31
00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:29.120
I always read and pictured things in motion.

32
00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:33.940
They were static, but I saw it all in motion.

33
00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:40.520
That is, some embryo of cinema was in me already then.

34
00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:48.240
Later, when I was already a lad of about 16–17,

35
00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.900
I often walked along Knez Mihailova street,

36
00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.400
which was then our main place for strolling,

37
00:04:55.480 --> 00:05:00.480
and in the present-day Academy of Sciences, below, on the ground floor,

38
00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:04.520
where there is a bookstore today, there used to be a "Cinefoto" store —

39
00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.360
of cameras, photo materials and so on.

40
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:16.680
In the window there I noticed a device that I didn't know what it was,

41
00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:20.400
and I simply fell in love with it.

42
00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:24.680
With it as a form, as a kind of aesthetic object.

43
00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:29.960
And every time I passed by there, I stopped and looked at this device.

44
00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:33.220
One day I dared to go inside and ask what it was.

45
00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:38.400
And the salesman told me it was a Russian amateur film camera,

46
00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:44.840
8 mm, brand "Kvarc," and at that moment I understood it had to be mine.

47
00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:52.840
And so, somehow at the same time, when they started building Šumice,

48
00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:58.020
the Šumice neighborhood, they demolished the house where we lived,

49
00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:00.760
gave us an apartment and a little money.

50
00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:05.680
And my mother asked my sister and me

51
00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:11.900
what we wanted her to buy us as a memento of this house.

52
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:15.420
I don't remember what my sister asked for, and I blurted out:

53
00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:20.880
a Russian film camera, 8 mm, "Kvarc,"

54
00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:24.800
and, of course, a projector was needed with it, and so on.

55
00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.900
She crossed herself, but bought it.

56
00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:32.960
And I shot my first footage precisely in this park,

57
00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.260
in Topčider Park.

58
00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:38.600
We were brought here with school on an excursion.

59
00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:42.400
They organized some cross-country race, I, of course, didn't run this race,

60
00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:43.400
but filmed.

61
00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:47.320
And then at school we watched these recordings,

62
00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:51.360
everyone was thrilled to see themselves on screen.

63
00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:56.700
And the recordings, of course, were pure beginner's work, clumsy,

64
00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:01.060
with some uncertain pans, now left, now right,

65
00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:04.040
now here, now there, I don't even know what I wanted,

66
00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:06.660
the camera shook in my hand and so on.

67
00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:13.700
And then a friend told me that he had read somewhere

68
00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:18.920
that somewhere on Boris Kidrič street, that's today's Beogradska street,

69
00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:22.500
by the law faculty, there's a "Kino Klub Beograd"

70
00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:29.340
and that they had just announced enrollment for courses for film amateurs.

71
00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:36.140
And I, of course, went there, signed up, and that's how it all began.

72
00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:39.980
After that I no longer controlled anything.

73
00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:48.890
I don't know, it came about somehow spontaneously.

74
00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:55.930
I got the chance to professionally shoot a documentary film

75
00:07:55.930 --> 00:08:01.890
right after I enrolled at the faculty, maybe in the first or second year,

76
00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:07.890
and for the whole of the former Yugoslavia at that,

77
00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:15.310
about some woman near Negotin, I think.

78
00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:20.450
That was, so to speak, my first encounter with documentary film,

79
00:08:20.510 --> 00:08:26.310
and here's what always attracted me in it: however you prepare for a shoot,

80
00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:28.570
there's a certain degree of the unknown.

81
00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:32.370
In the sense of whether what you planned will succeed,

82
00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:35.750
whether everything will really happen that way,

83
00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:38.330
and something unforeseen always happens.

84
00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:43.970
So in this sense documentary film is a challenge, isn't it?

85
00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:51.990
And the challenge is to do nothing false,

86
00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:56.890
staged, forced,

87
00:08:56.970 --> 00:09:02.050
but for there really to be that degree, a large degree of conviction.

88
00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:13.070
Which is at the same time the most difficult form of documentary film.

89
00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:17.610
There's a certain boundary here, not very tangible:

90
00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:21.270
what is a report and what is a documentary film,

91
00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:24.490
what is some more engaged report and so on.

92
00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:29.870
So, maybe the best boundary: as little talking as possible

93
00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:36.930
straight into the camera and more immersion in the idea and topic

94
00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:39.790
you've become attached to, for which you make

95
00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:42.970
a documentary film at all — more through image and sound,

96
00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:47.870
not through monologue, dialogue, voice-over and so on.

97
00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:52.050
These, in my opinion, are the purest documentary films,

98
00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:55.130
although they are also the hardest to make,

99
00:09:55.130 --> 00:09:58.370
because the whole idea has to be told only through image

100
00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:01.170
and, maybe, some noise, sound.

101
00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:15.580
It is our custom and culture to write history textbooks,

102
00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:19.020
keep diaries, photograph beautiful moments,

103
00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:21.720
document stories. Why?

104
00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:26.020
Do we simply document the time and social reality

105
00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:26.880
in which we live?

106
00:10:27.340 --> 00:10:30.100
Do we leave something to future generations?

107
00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:33.340
Or are we simply afraid of oblivion?

108
00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:44.570
The creative process is as important to me

109
00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:46.770
as the result itself, that is, the film.

110
00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:51.210
Because on the way from the idea to the final version of the film

111
00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:57.230
you are present at the birth of something new,

112
00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:58.470
something that exists nowhere else.

113
00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:01.690
It's like a plant,

114
00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:05.730
whose seed you plant in the ground, and before your eyes

115
00:11:05.730 --> 00:11:08.970
from day to day, from week to week, from month to month

116
00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:14.990
a plant grows that you will pick and share with someone.

117
00:11:15.370 --> 00:11:16.350
It's the same with a film.

118
00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:21.530
This film and this process itself actually enriches us,

119
00:11:21.530 --> 00:11:25.630
definitely changes us — me, and the team, and the heroes themselves,

120
00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:29.670
because, on the one hand, we learn something new,

121
00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:34.170
live through an experience we didn't have before,

122
00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:36.890
or experience it differently,

123
00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:40.730
and this undoubtedly enriches us emotionally.

124
00:11:41.750 --> 00:11:45.770
It seems to me that if this process itself

125
00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:52.980
doesn't affect me somehow as a director,

126
00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:58.470
I'm not sure I'll manage with this film too,

127
00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:00.410
as the result of this process,

128
00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:04.390
to influence the audience or move it.

129
00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:10.310
Every decision I make is very important to me,

130
00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:14.950
and every moment — from the first minute when the idea comes to me

131
00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:18.190
to make a film, up to the last export

132
00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:19.750
of the last version of the film.

133
00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:22.790
It is exceptionally important, like the film itself.

134
00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:27.550
On the other hand, the film itself is interesting

135
00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:33.790
and should help us understand

136
00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:37.950
the reality around us, look at it

137
00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:40.990
differently, make us think

138
00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:49.870
and encourage us to make our life better in quality and, of course, better.

139
00:14:09.260 --> 00:14:16.640
The film has been shown at numerous festivals around the world,

140
00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:24.680
from IDFA, one of the most prestigious documentary film festivals in the world,

141
00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:31.920
through festivals in Egypt, in New Zealand — to our domestic festivals, the March Festival.

142
00:14:33.270 --> 00:14:38.180
This film also received many awards, about ten awards,

143
00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:46.920
but what matters no less for this film is that we managed one universal theme —

144
00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:57.500
the relativity of human freedom — to tell through the life of convicts in several prisons in Serbia,

145
00:14:57.500 --> 00:15:08.420
and that this story was recognized, understood, read both by the audience in New Zealand

146
00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:17.680
and by our domestic audience, and that the audience recognized the emotion we wanted to convey with this film.

147
00:15:28.730 --> 00:15:34.330
I was never interested in awards. I enjoy my film while I'm editing it,

148
00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:39.810
and then I don't like it. I don't like any of my films, not one, there's no such,

149
00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:44.410
but I enjoy each one and would give my life for it — until the moment I finish editing.

150
00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:50.170
And countless times when I was obliged to attend a festival where my film is shown,

151
00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:54.870
I start the film and run out into the street, and return only when it's all over.

152
00:15:55.210 --> 00:16:00.630
I don't like going back to my film. Many authors value it, we're different after all.

153
00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:08.570
I just look: at that moment it was what I could do,

154
00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:13.050
I did it, and after that I have no desire to return to the subject.

155
00:16:14.210 --> 00:16:18.370
There are films that are connected with me by some other threads,

156
00:16:18.490 --> 00:16:22.450
and to say that this is the best or worst film... no idea what the measure is here,

157
00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:24.130
it's silly to measure anything.

158
00:16:24.130 --> 00:16:30.650
But some films have stayed with me, they're deeper, so I can't,

159
00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:35.130
how to put it, forget them, that is, can't tear myself away from them.

160
00:16:35.770 --> 00:16:41.910
Say, I can say it's, for example, "Keepers of Silence,"

161
00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:51.110
a film about which I can say: it's the only film I'm completely satisfied with,

162
00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:55.510
because I accomplished what I had planned, and not only accomplished what I planned,

163
00:16:55.610 --> 00:17:01.250
but it turned out, I don't know by what percent, more than what I planned.

164
00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:05.210
And that is what makes me happy.

165
00:17:05.410 --> 00:17:09.690
There are films that received big awards, and I'm dissatisfied with those films.

166
00:17:09.690 --> 00:17:13.130
For the simple reason that I didn't accomplish what I wanted to accomplish.

167
00:17:13.570 --> 00:17:17.110
And they're successful. They say: how can you be dissatisfied?

168
00:17:17.110 --> 00:17:20.870
No, I'm dissatisfied, because I didn't do in it what I wanted to do.

169
00:17:20.930 --> 00:17:27.030
But "Keepers of Silence," for example, is a film in which I got more than I expected.

170
00:17:27.130 --> 00:17:31.510
Maybe for that reason, and I'm not saying it's a good, or less good, or bad film, that doesn't matter.

171
00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:37.810
There are films that I, say, shot almost exactly according to the director's script.

172
00:17:37.810 --> 00:17:41.690
24 frames, say, the film "Kolo" — I shot 24 frames.

173
00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:43.590
Am I satisfied with this film? No.

174
00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:49.770
Now it seems to me sterile, because everything is polished, 24 frames.

175
00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:53.230
And this film also received some big awards and so on,

176
00:17:53.230 --> 00:17:58.250
but I'm dissatisfied, because I believe the documentary form shouldn't be done this way.

177
00:17:58.390 --> 00:18:04.430
Because it's completely under control. Why didn't I give it a little lightness, why didn't I give it a bit more life and so on.

178
00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:36.720
Stories differ. Some stuck in my memory because of the exceptional effort needed just to arrive at the final result.

179
00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:41.700
Both physical and mental. In some extreme conditions.

180
00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:55.440
Like the one we shot before the bombings of '99, in January, at minus 17, three days in some hut.

181
00:19:56.240 --> 00:20:02.000
And from this side this film is exceptionally dear to me. I don't know if I could endure that now, at my age.

182
00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:08.260
Because it was exceptionally hard, but we ourselves wanted this effort — for everything to happen at precisely that time,

183
00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:14.500
in a storm and minus 17, somewhere at an altitude of 1700 meters.

184
00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:21.980
And I'm glad this shows in the film. This effort is visible in the film, and it's very authentic.

185
00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:38.140
Some other film I would perhaps remember for its value, which is felt even today, 25 years later.

186
00:20:38.140 --> 00:20:45.560
There, it's long, over an hour, it tells about the time between 1994 and 1995.

187
00:20:45.740 --> 00:20:49.780
Those very worst times here in our parts.

188
00:20:49.960 --> 00:21:00.040
And when everything became problematic. This is the film "Geto," which is 90% documentary.

189
00:21:00.040 --> 00:21:08.820
There are scenes that are slightly staged, but overall it's an exceptional document of that time.

190
00:21:09.460 --> 00:21:15.140
And recently a person came up to me who graduated from us a long time ago.

191
00:21:15.400 --> 00:21:22.300
And says: professor, I watched it for the fifth time, I watched "Geto" again, he says, in 25 years nothing has changed.

192
00:21:22.300 --> 00:21:28.640
That was 1995, so from this angle the film is exceptionally valuable.

193
00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:35.380
And I can single it out because, I think, it will always be valuable whenever someone takes it out of a drawer

194
00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:43.060
and watches it — they'll understand, very deeply understand, what was going on in those years.